{
  "stage": "SOURCE_INDEPENDENT_BEFORE_VIEWS_OR_AUTHOR",
  "seed_file_sha256": "17c2282c00838f8ec6c7e7cb9fcd6efbe91b610fffb5d95af72a9475038e20d6",
  "concept_blocks": [1, 2, 16, 25, 26],
  "definition_observations": "A system solution satisfies every equation: it is the intersection, not a union or a solution of an arbitrarily chosen subset. Substitution applies to linear systems and to a quadratic plus a linear equation; two quadratics need special structure.",
  "images": [],
  "author_or_views_read": false,
  "sources": [
    {"number":"102","tasks":6,"solution":"a: substitute x=3y+5 to obtain 26y=-26, hence (2,-1). b: -34y-7=146 gives y=-9/2 and x=8. c: y=12x-16 gives 41x=123, hence (3,20). d: eliminate y to get 71x=568, hence (8,-9). e: elimination gives 43x=430, hence (10,1). f: elimination gives 5x=25, hence (5,4). Each ordered pair satisfies both original equations. Choice recognition cannot demonstrate actually executing substitution."},
    {"number":"103","tasks":9,"solution":"a: y^2-4y+3=0 gives (3,1),(5,3). b: 3y^2+8y-3=0 gives (3,1/3),(-7,-3). c: x(x-5y)=10 and x-5y=1 give (10,9/5). d: x=(y+3)/2 gives 3y^2+15y-18=0, hence (2,1),(-3/2,-6). e: x=(2+2y)/3 yields 13y^2+8y-896=0 with discriminant 216^2, giving (6,8),(-66/13,-112/13). f: x=3y/2 gives y^2=16, hence (6,4),(-6,-4). g: (2x-3y)(2x+3y)=15 gives 2x-3y=3, hence (2,1/3). h: x=2y+1 gives 5y^2+5y-10=0, hence (3,1),(-3,-2). i: y=(-1-3x)/2 gives x^2-8x+15=0, hence (3,-5),(5,-8). Complete sets must retain both quadratic roots and order, including negative and fractional results."},
    {"number":"104","tasks":3,"solution":"a: y=x-5 gives 2x(x-5)=0, intersections (0,-5),(5,0), exactly two. b: (x+y)^2=4 and xy=-8 give sums 2 or -2, producing (4,-2),(-2,4),(2,-4),(-4,2), exactly four. c: t=x^2>=0 gives t+(t/2-10)^2=100, so t(t-36)=0. Intersections (0,-10),(-6,8),(6,8), exactly three. At (0,-10) both circle and parabola have horizontal tangents; contact is an intersection even without crossing. The written source requires graphical solution; coordinates/count/tangency recognition can retain intersection mathematics but not graph construction or visual intersection finding."},
    {"number":"105","tasks":2,"solution":"a: first two equations force (5,-5), but x^2-y^2=0, not 40; the intersection of all three sets is empty. b: first two force (1,0), but x^2+y^2=1, not 2; again empty. Satisfying two equations is not enough."},
    {"number":"106","tasks":4,"solution":"All four reciprocal systems require x!=0 and y!=0. a: x+y=10 implies (x+y)/(xy)=5/12, thus xy=24 and (4,6),(6,4). b: (x-y)/(xy)=3/20 gives xy=40 and x-y=6, hence (10,4),(-4,-10). c: 1.2=6/5; y=5-2x gives 12x^2-35x+25=0, hence (5/3,5/3),(5/4,5/2). d: 0.1=1/10; y=2x+3 gives 2x^2-7x-30=0, hence (6,15),(-5/2,-2). Multiplying denominators does not authorize zero-valued coordinates; negative solutions are legal."},
    {"number":"107","tasks":2,"solution":"a: t^2-20t+96=(t-8)(t-12), so the numbers are 8 and 12. b: designate x-y=6 and xy=216. y^2+6y-216=(y-12)(y+18), giving ordered pairs (18,12),(-12,-18); unordered number pairs {12,18} and {-18,-12}. No positivity restriction excludes the negative pair."},
    {"number":"108","tasks":2,"solution":"a: positive sides satisfy a+b=41 and a^2+b^2=29^2=841, so ab=420 and sides 20m,21m. b: the hypotenuse is 37cm, so positive legs sum 84-37=47cm and squares sum 1369cm^2. Their product is (47^2-1369)/2=420cm^2, giving legs 12cm,35cm and area ab/2=210cm^2. Hypotenuse is not a leg."},
    {"number":"109","tasks":2,"solution":"a: original perimeter 136m becomes 92m, so new side sum is 46m; half area is 240m^2. Roots t^2-46t+240=0 are 40m,6m. New width must not exceed 8m and new length must not exceed 60m, excluding reversed assignment. Decreases: length20m,width2m. b: let x receive +8m and y receive +2m. x+y=40; (x+8)(y+2)=3xy/2 gives xy=4x+16y+32. Setting x=40-y gives y^2-28y+192=0, so (x,y)=(28,12) or (24,16), in meters. Products increase from336 to504 and384 to576 respectively; +8 attaches to the longer side in both. The roles cannot be reversed without changing the equation."},
    {"number":"110","tasks":2,"solution":"a: number n and digit sum s satisfy n+2s=96, ns=952. Eliminating n gives s^2-48s+476=0, roots14,34. Since a two-digit number has digit sum at most18, s=14 and n=68; its digits actually sum14. b: with tens a, units b, unchanged digits after +27 must reverse because the number changes, so 10a+b+27=10b+a and b-a=3. a^2+b^2=65 gives a^2+3a-28=0, a=4 or-7; legal digits give a4,b7 and number47, reversed74."},
    {"number":"111","tasks":2,"solution":"a: positive speeds u fromA and v fromB have u+v=28km/h. A traveller arrives later, so 28/u-28/v=35/60=7/12h. This gives u12,v16km/h, with travel times7/3h and7/4h; the other algebraic root u112,v-84 is inadmissible. b: positive speeds u fromM and v fromN satisfy u+v=45/(20/60)=135km/h; M train arrives earlier, so 45/v-45/u=9/60=3/20h. Speeds u75,v60km/h give arrivals36min and45min. The other root v675,u-540 is inadmissible. Swapping role labels reverses the arrival statement."},
    {"number":"112","tasks":2,"solution":"a: individual full-tank times T,U>0 obey 1/T+1/U=1/12 and T/2+U/2=25, so T+U=50, TU=600, roots20min,30min. Halfwork uses half each individual time, not a sum of rates. b: faster full-job time t and slower t+24 satisfy 1/t+1/(t+24)=1/35, giving t^2-46t-840=0. Roots60,-14; positive times are60h,84h. Combined rates, not individual completion times, add."},
    {"number":"113","tasks":3,"solution":"(5,1) gives25+15+1=41 and (1,5) gives1+15+25=41, so both are solutions. For arbitrary real a,b with a^2+3ab+b^2=41, commutativity gives b^2+3ba+a^2=a^2+3ab+b^2=41. This identity proves swapped pairs work universally; two examples alone cannot establish that universal implication."},
    {"number":"114","tasks":2,"solution":"a: principal sqrt12=2sqrt3 and sqrt12*sqrt3=6, hence x^2+xy+y^2=12+6+3=21, not29; false. b: squared terms sum22, product (3+sqrt2)(3-sqrt2)=7, hence total29; true."},
    {"number":"115","tasks":2,"solution":"a: real domain x>=0,y>=0 and principal radicals imply sqrt x=1+sqrt y; all solutions (x,y)=((t+1)^2,t^2) for t>=0, for example (1,0),(4,1). b: original domain y>=0,x+y>=0, and sqrt(x+y)=1+sqrt y imply x=1+2sqrt y. All solutions (1+2t,t^2), t>=0, for example (1,0),(3,1). Any two distinct legal pairs suffice; a full set is not required by the source. Negative signed-root parameters and squaring without checking the original radical equation can introduce extraneous pairs."},
    {"number":"116","tasks":4,"solution":"Open quadrants exclude axes. a: y=x^2+4>0, so I andII; also point(0,4) on the y-axis. b: y=9-x^2 is positive for |x|<3 and negative for |x|>3, so I,II,III,IV; also axes points(0,9),(3,0),(-3,0). c: xy=6>0 forces same nonzero sign, so I,III and no axes. d: xy=-12<0 forces opposite nonzero signs, so II,IV and no axes. No graphs are required here."}
  ]
}
